output.generality:
This helps any table scan whose pushed filters (constant predicates, join min/max or Bloom filters, or exact bitmap filters) let through less than about 12% of rows per vector, when the scan feeds hash joins, projections or aggregates. That is common in star-schema queries where small filtered dimension tables push join filters into a large fact table. Because the cost being saved is per chunk, every operator downstream benefits.

It does not apply to:
- unfiltered scans, which stay zero-copy;
- scans passing more than 12.5% of rows per vector;
- index scans and CREATE INDEX scans;
- transaction-local storage chunks from a different batch, which are never mixed in.

Possible downsides are an extra copy of the surviving rows (only for sparse chunks), and LIMIT queries without ORDER BY may read a few more vectors per call.
